      {
        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 10,
        "titulo": "Mein, dein, sein…: possessives",
        "explicacao": "Possessives agree with the THING owned (gender and number), in their ending: «mein Vater», «meine Mutter», «mein Kind», «meine Eltern». They take the same endings as «ein»: no ending for masculine and neuter, -e for feminine and plural. The stem comes from the owner, as in English: «sein» = his (or its), «ihr» = her (and their); «Ihr», capitalized, is the formal \"your\". Careful: «unser» and «euer» lose the inner -e- before an ending: unsere, eure.",
